1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,880
Tutor: So now I want you to discuss the lesson we've just been watching on the video

2
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:10,080
and think about the ways in which origami can be a useful educational tool.

3
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:13,860
Can you all work with the person sitting next to you...

4
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:23,200
Seb: I had no idea that such a simple thing like folding squares of paper to make the shape of something like a bird could be such an amazing tool.

5
00:00:24,120 --> 00:00:26,400
It's made me see origami in a whole new light.

6
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:33,580
Lia: I know. It was interesting to see the educational skills the children were developing by doing origami.

7
00:00:33,620 --> 00:00:40,740
On the video you could see them really listening hard to make sure they did all the steps in the right order to make the bird.

8
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:44,840
Seb: That's right. In this lesson they were working individually

9
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:47,080
but it would also be interesting to see

10
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:52,880
if the children could work out how to make something simple without being given any direction.

11
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:55,860
That would help with building teamwork as well.

12
00:00:55,860 --> 00:00:59,240
Lia: Yes, but much more of a challenge.

13
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:03,900
One thing that really stood out for me was that the children were all having fun

14
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:05,700
while being taught something new.

15
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:08,600
Seb: Which is a key aim of any lesson with this age group.

16
00:01:09,260 --> 00:01:12,820
And although these kids had no problems with folding the paper,

17
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:17,860
with younger children you could do origami to help practise fine motor skills.

18
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:23,960
Lia: Absolutely. Shall we talk about the individual children we saw on the video?

19
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:27,640
I wrote all their names down and took some notes.

20
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:28,820
Seb: Yes, I did too.

21
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:32,460
Lia: OK, good. Let's start with Sid.

22
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:36,780
Seb: He was interesting because before they started doing the origami

23
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:39,020
he was being quite disruptive.

24
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:43,540
Lia: Yes. He really benefited from having to use his hands -

25
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:47,460
it helped him to settle down and start concentrating.

26
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:51,620
Seb: Yes, I noticed that too. What about Jack

27
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,920
I noticed he seemed to want to work things out for himself.

28
00:01:56,920 --> 00:02:01,380
Lia: Mmm. You could see him trying out different things rather than asking the teacher for help.

29
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:03,980
What did you make of Naomi?

30
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:07,420
Seb: She seemed to be losing interest at one point

31
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:11,340
but then she decided she wanted her mouse to be the best

32
00:02:11,340 --> 00:02:13,860
and that motivated her to try harder.

33
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:17,360
Lia: She didn't seem satisfied with hers in the end, though.

34
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:18,160
Seb: No.

35
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:21,420
Lia: Anya was such a star.

36
00:02:21,860 --> 00:02:24,600
She listened so carefully and

37
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:28,500
then produced the perfect bird with very little effort.

38
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:33,120
Seb: Mmm - I think the teacher could have increased the level of difficulty for her.

39
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:38,980
Lia: Maybe. I think it was the first time Zara had come across origami.

40
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:42,200
Seb: She looked as if she didn't really get what was going on.

41
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:45,720
Lia: She seemed unsure about what she was supposed to do,

42
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:48,780
but in the end hers didn't turn out too badly.

43
00:02:49,420 --> 00:02:52,620
Seb: Yeah. I'm sure it was a positive learning experience for her.

44
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:57,840
Lia: I think one reason why the origami activity worked

45
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:02,060
so well in this class was that the teacher was well prepared.

46
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:06,380
Seb: Right. I think it would have taken me ages to prepare examples,

47
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:09,660
showing each of the steps involved in making the bird.

48
00:03:09,660 --> 00:03:15,200
But that was a really good idea. The children could see what they were aiming for -

49
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:18,200
and much better for them to be able to hold something

50
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:20,560
rather than just looking at pictures.

51
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:25,560
Lia: Mmm - those physical examples supported her verbal explanations really well.

52
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:31,020
Seb: It's strange that origami isn't used more widely. Why do you think that is?

53
00:03:31,720 --> 00:03:35,000
Lia: Well, teachers may just feel it's not that appealing to children

54
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,880
who are used to doing everything on computers, especially boys.

55
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:41,160
Even if they're aware of the benefits.

56
00:03:42,010 --> 00:03:42,680
Seb: Oh, I don't know.

57
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:48,100
It's no different to any other craft activity. I bet it's

58
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:49,680
because so many teachers are clumsy like me.

59
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:54,240
Lia: That's true - too much effort required if you're not good with your hands.

60
00:03:54,640 --> 00:04:00,440
Seb: Well, anyway, I think we should try it out in our maths teaching practice with Year 3.

61
00:04:00,980 --> 00:04:08,100
I can see using origami is a really engaging way of reinforcing children's knowledge of geometric shapes,

62
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:10,080
like they were doing in the video,

63
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:15,240
but I think it would also work really well for presenting fractions

64
00:04:15,580 --> 00:04:16,920
which is coming up soon.

65
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:21,700
Lia: Good idea - that's something most of the kids in that class might struggle with.

66
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:25,540
Origami would also be good practice for using symmetry

67
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:28,040
but I think they did that last term.

68
00:04:28,360 --> 00:04:33,680
Seb: OK - well let's try and get some ideas together and plan the lesson next week.

69
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:36,400
Tutor: OK, if you could all stop...
